# CReplxx — vendored replxx

Upstream: https://github.com/AmokHuginnsson/replxx
Pinned commit: 1f149bfe20bf6e49c1afd4154eaf0032c8c2fda2 (DEPENDENCIES.md candidate [S09])
Licence: replxx/LICENSE.md (BSD-style, copyright retained, unmodified)
C++ standard: C++11 (upstream CMakeLists); threads via libpthread.

Files in `replxx/` are upstream sources from the pinned commit
(headers, .cxx sources, ConvertUTF.cpp, wcwidth.cpp), PLUS the
registered local patches below. Only these bridge/support files are
Needletail's: `include/shim.h`, `include/module.modulemap`,
`SHA256SUMS-upstream`, `patches/*.patch`, this file.

Provenance model (tree == upstream + registered patches):
- `SHA256SUMS-upstream` records the pristine upstream blob hashes.
- `patches/` holds each local deviation as a reviewed unified diff
  (one file per patch, repo-rooted a/ b/ paths). No other tree edit
  under `replxx/` is permitted.
- `./dev vendor-check` verifies both directions per patch: the tree
  file minus the patch hashes to the upstream manifest entry, and the
  patch applied to those pristine bytes reproduces the tree file
  byte-for-byte. The upstream manifest itself is never rewritten to
  bless a tree edit.
- Upstream ref `ThirdParty/replxx` (a full clone used for the
  secondary comparison) is gitignored and never committed.

## Registered patches (each independently reviewed)

1. `patches/0001-bracketed-paste-decode-lf.patch` →
   `replxx/replxx_impl.cxx` (two hunks).
   (a) `bracketed_paste`: decode pasted `KEY::control('J')` (LF) back
   to `'\n'`. Terminal::read_char control-maps C0 bytes; the paste
   path converted CR and TAB but not LF, so a pasted newline arrived
   as the out-of-range key code 0x200004A and UTF-8-encoded as U+FFFD
   on output — multiline pastes lost every line break (found by M4-D
   PTY-02 development evidence). Mirrors the adjacent CR/TAB
   handling, same style. Required by COMPLETE-SPEC PTY-02 (exact
   submitted text).
   (b) `read_char` coalesced-wakeup drain (M4-MAC-04B notification
   backpressure): after draining pending messages on a MESSAGE wakeup,
   re-check the keypress queue and break to deliver it immediately.
   Wakeup tokens are type-tagged, but when the pipe is full the
   nonblocking notifier (0002) drops tokens of ANY type; a keypress
   whose token was dropped behind a message token must still be
   delivered on that wake, or it is delayed until an unrelated event.
   Messages and resizes self-drain (any pending token wakes
   wait_for_input immediately); only the keypress case needs the
   explicit re-check. Required by the M4-MAC-04B saturation proof
   (the writer stays responsive with the wakeup channel saturated).

Swift boundary: `shim.h` exposes only the C API (`extern "C"` in
replxx/replxx.h). C++ interoperability does not cross into Swift.

### Registered patch 0002

`patches/0002-darwin-terminal-transitions.patch` → `replxx/terminal.cxx`:
Darwin raw-mode transitions use TCSANOW (TCSADRAIN livelocks against a
concurrent renderer on a pty — the M4-MAC-02/03 exit hangs) and retry
tcsetattr on EINTR (a swallowed signal-interrupted restore left the
terminal raw forever — the in-suite exit nondeterminism). The wakeup
pipe's write end is nonblocking and `notify_event` drops its token on
EAGAIN: the pipe is a wakeup channel only, a full pipe means an unread
token is already pending, and the reader drains messages, keypresses
and resizes on any wake (0001(b)) — so a dropped token loses nothing,
while a blocking write is the output-thread/input-thread circular
wait (M4-MAC-04B: the notification-pipe backpressure fixed at its
source). Verified both directions by `./dev vendor-check`.
